-- CONSIDER-on-enum and TYPICALLY, in one exhibit.
--
-- `unit rate` scrutinises an enumeration, which Catala renders as a `match`
-- over an `enumeration` (§4.3). `tariff payable` carries a `TYPICALLY` on one
-- of its GIVEN parameters, which R10 lowers to a Catala `context` variable
-- plus an in-scope `definition` of the default — so a Catala caller may omit
-- the cap where an L4 caller must still supply it. That divergence is
-- disclosed in the emitted document's notes block.

IMPORT prelude

DECLARE `Customer class` IS ONE OF
    domestic
    commercial
    industrial

DECLARE Account HAS
    class     IS A `Customer class`
    unitsUsed IS A NUMBER

@export The per-unit rate for an account's customer class.
GIVEN a IS AN Account
GIVETH A NUMBER
`unit rate` a MEANS
    CONSIDER a's class
      WHEN domestic   THEN 0.20
      WHEN commercial THEN 0.35
      WHEN industrial THEN 0.50

@export Tariff payable on metered consumption, capped.
GIVEN a   IS AN Account
      cap IS A NUMBER TYPICALLY 500
GIVETH A NUMBER
`tariff payable` a cap MEANS
    min cap (a's unitsUsed TIMES `unit rate` a)

#EVAL `tariff payable` (Account WITH class IS commercial, unitsUsed IS 1000) 500
#EVAL `tariff payable` (Account WITH class IS domestic, unitsUsed IS 100) 500
-- The cap BINDS here (2000 units at 0.50 is 1000, over the 500 cap), which is
-- what makes the emitted `definition cap equals 500.0` observable: the twin
-- test scope R10 emits for this directive omits `cap`, so a wrong default
-- would change its answer. With a non-binding cap it would not.
#EVAL `tariff payable` (Account WITH class IS industrial, unitsUsed IS 2000) 500

#EVAL `unit rate` (Account WITH class IS industrial, unitsUsed IS 0)
